// Generated by packages/academy/scripts/build-content.ts. Don't edit by hand.
export default JSON.parse("{\"1.8.1\":{\"unit\":\"1.8\",\"courseId\":\"1.8.1\",\"bridge\":\"Unit 1.7 ended with rules that hold under pressure. In 1.7.3, six doubled bets cost 6,300.00 against 600.00 at a fixed size; in 0.2.2, a game that won nine times in ten still lost. Here doubling gets its name: martingale.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Start with a bet of 1 unit and double it after each loss. After 7 losses in a row, how big is the next bet?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"14 units\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"64 units\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"128 units\"}],\"answer\":\"c\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. The bets were 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 and 64: 127 already lost. The next one is 128, to win back 127 and make 1.\",\"concept\":\"martingale\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Doubling after losses\",\"text\":\"[[martingale|Martingale]] means doubling the stake after every loss, so the first win recovers all the losses plus the first stake. It wins small almost every time, which makes it feel safe. [[averaging-down|Averaging down]], adding to a losing position to lower its average price, works the same way: it's martingale in disguise.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The doubling table\",\"lines\":[\"Bets after each loss: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64.\",\"Lost so far = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + 32 + 64 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":127,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":128,\"text\":\"128 is the next bet. Add only the seven bets already lost.\"},{\"value\":64,\"text\":\"64 is the last bet lost. Add all seven.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 127 lost, and the next bet is 128, to win back 127 and make 1.\"},\"after\":\"Each loss doubles the next bet. After 10 losses the bet is 1,024 times the first, and 1,023 times the first is already gone.\",\"concept\":\"martingale\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"A martingale bot\",\"text\":\"A bot bets 10.00 on fair coin flips from 10,000.00 and doubles after every loss.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":10000,\"h\":10010,\"l\":10000,\"c\":10010},{\"o\":10010,\"h\":10020,\"l\":10000,\"c\":10020},{\"o\":10020,\"h\":10030,\"l\":10020,\"c\":10030},{\"o\":10030,\"h\":10040,\"l\":10000,\"c\":10040},{\"o\":10040,\"h\":10050,\"l\":10040,\"c\":10050},{\"o\":10050,\"h\":10060,\"l\":10050,\"c\":10060},{\"o\":10060,\"h\":10070,\"l\":10050,\"c\":10070},{\"o\":10070,\"h\":10080,\"l\":10000,\"c\":10080},{\"o\":10080,\"h\":10090,\"l\":10080,\"c\":10090},{\"o\":10090,\"h\":10100,\"l\":10080,\"c\":10100},{\"o\":10100,\"h\":10110,\"l\":10100,\"c\":10110},{\"o\":10110,\"h\":10120,\"l\":10110,\"c\":10120},{\"o\":10120,\"h\":10130,\"l\":10090,\"c\":10130},{\"o\":10130,\"h\":10140,\"l\":10120,\"c\":10140},{\"o\":10140,\"h\":10150,\"l\":10140,\"c\":10150},{\"o\":10150,\"h\":10160,\"l\":10000,\"c\":10160},{\"o\":10160,\"h\":10170,\"l\":10160,\"c\":10170},{\"o\":10170,\"h\":10180,\"l\":10160,\"c\":10180},{\"o\":10180,\"h\":10190,\"l\":10180,\"c\":10190},{\"o\":10190,\"h\":10190,\"l\":5080,\"c\":5080}],\"visible\":19,\"describe\":\"Twenty betting cycles of a martingale bot on fair coin flips. Equity creeps from 10,000.00 to 10,190.00 over 19 cycles, then falls to 5,080.00 in one cycle of nine losses.\"},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"Each candle is one cycle: losses, then a win. Nineteen cycles, nineteen wins of 10.00.\",\"visible\":19,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":10000,\"role\":\"neutral\",\"label\":\"Start 10,000.00\"}]},{\"text\":\"Cycle 20: nine losses in a row. 10 + 20 + … + 2,560 = 5,110.00 gone.\",\"visible\":20,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"bracket\",\"from\":5080,\"to\":10190,\"index\":19,\"label\":\"−5,110.00\",\"role\":\"down\"}]},{\"text\":\"5,080.00 is left. The next bet, 5,120.00, doesn't fit. The bot is finished.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":5080,\"role\":\"stop\",\"label\":\"Left 5,080.00\"}]}]},\"concept\":\"martingale\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Why it can't win\",\"text\":\"Doubling can't beat a fair game: each cycle's expected result is zero, and costs make it negative. Losing runs are rare on any one bet but common over many. At 50%, a run of 8 losses turns up in 17% of 100-flip stretches. Betting red in European roulette, a run of 6 losses turns up in 84% of 200-spin sessions.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Averaging down\",\"lines\":[\"Buy 50 at 100.00, stop planned at 98.00: 1R = 100.00 before costs.\",\"At 98.00, instead of exiting, buy 50 more. Average price: 99.00.\",\"Price falls to 96.00. Loss = 100 units × 3.00 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":300,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":200,\"text\":\"That's the first 50 units only. All 100 units lost 3.00 each from the 99.00 average.\"},{\"value\":100,\"text\":\"100.00 was the plan, 1R. Averaging down made the loss bigger.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 300.00: 3R, three times what the plan allowed, on an idea that was already wrong.\"},\"after\":\"A lower average price feels like progress. It's a bigger position on a losing idea, with the stop gone.\",\"concept\":\"averaging-down\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Split entry or averaging down?\",\"prompt\":\"Sort each case. A planned split entry fixes the total risk before the first order; averaging down adds risk after the idea starts failing.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"50 units planned in two halves, one stop for both, total risk 1R\",\"right\":\"Planned split entry\"},{\"left\":\"Adding to a loser because it's 'cheaper now'\",\"right\":\"Averaging down\"},{\"left\":\"Second half bought at 99.00 as the plan said, stop unchanged\",\"right\":\"Planned split entry\"},{\"left\":\"Cancelling the stop and buying more to lower the average\",\"right\":\"Averaging down\"}],\"columns\":[\"Case\",\"Type\"],\"correct\":\"Right. The difference isn't buying lower; it's whether the total risk was fixed before you started. Scaling in to a plan is allowed (2.7). Averaging down is grade F.\",\"concept\":\"averaging-down\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Before you run it\",\"prompt\":\"The bot from the figure plays 1,000 fair flips. A run of 9 losses would cost it 5,110.00 in one sequence. What's the chance that run turns up at least once?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"About 1 in 50\",\"feedback\":\"Any 9 particular flips all lose 1 time in 512. But 1,000 flips give that run hundreds of chances to start.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"About 1 in 10\",\"feedback\":\"Higher. Each flip can start a new run, and there are 1,000 of them.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"About 1 in 3\",\"feedback\":\"Higher still. Even a run of 10 has a 38.5% chance; a run of 9 is more likely than not.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"More likely than not\"}],\"answer\":\"d\"},\"reveal\":\"62.4%, calculated exactly. A long losing run is rare on any one flip but likely over many flips, and the capital is finite. Paths that escape show small, steady gains. On average the result is zero before costs and negative after.\",\"concept\":\"martingale\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Doubling after losses and averaging down without a limit lead to a [[blow-up]] by construction: grade F. No skill fixes the maths. Keep the risk per trade fixed, win or lose.\"}],\"check\":[\"q-1.8.1-1\",\"q-1.8.1-2\",\"q-1.8.1-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"After 7 losses, a doubling bet is 128 times the first, with 127 already lost.\",\"A high win rate can hide one ruinous loss: a run of 9 losses in 1,000 flips is likely.\",\"A split entry fixes total risk before the first order. Averaging down adds risk after.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 1.8.2: grid trading, averaging down on a schedule, and what one trend does to it.\"}},\"1.8.2\":{\"unit\":\"1.8\",\"courseId\":\"1.8.2\",\"bridge\":\"1.8.1 showed averaging down: adding to a loser after the idea starts failing. A grid does the same on a schedule. In 1.4.7 you measured exposure as a share of the account; here you watch it grow, unit by unit, as price trends away.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"A grid bot buys 10 units at every 1.00 step below 100.00 and sells each at +1.00. It has won 40 trades in a row. Now price falls to 90.00 without a bounce. How many units does it hold?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"10 units\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"50 units\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"100 units\"}],\"answer\":\"c\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. It bought 10 units at each of 99.00, 98.00 … 90.00: 100 units, and none could be sold 1.00 higher.\",\"concept\":\"grid-trading\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"How a grid works\",\"text\":\"A [[grid-trading|grid]] places orders at fixed steps around a price, often with no stop. Each buy has a sell 1.00 above it. In a range, price keeps crossing the steps and lots close for small wins. When price moves away and keeps going, the grid keeps buying: [[exposure]] grows exactly when the idea is failing.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Range, then trend\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100.2,\"h\":100.35,\"l\":99.7,\"c\":99.8},{\"o\":99.8,\"h\":99.9,\"l\":98.9,\"c\":99.05},{\"o\":99.05,\"h\":99.6,\"l\":99,\"c\":99.5},{\"o\":99.5,\"h\":100.1,\"l\":99.4,\"c\":100.05},{\"o\":100.05,\"h\":100.2,\"l\":99.3,\"c\":99.4},{\"o\":99.4,\"h\":99.5,\"l\":98.85,\"c\":98.95},{\"o\":98.95,\"h\":98.98,\"l\":97.9,\"c\":98.1},{\"o\":98.1,\"h\":99.1,\"l\":98.05,\"c\":99},{\"o\":99,\"h\":99.9,\"l\":98.95,\"c\":99.85},{\"o\":99.85,\"h\":100.2,\"l\":99.7,\"c\":100.15},{\"o\":100.15,\"h\":100.3,\"l\":99.55,\"c\":99.6},{\"o\":99.6,\"h\":100.25,\"l\":99.5,\"c\":100.2},{\"o\":100.2,\"h\":100.28,\"l\":99.33,\"c\":99.4},{\"o\":99.4,\"h\":99.48,\"l\":98.53,\"c\":98.6},{\"o\":98.6,\"h\":98.68,\"l\":97.68,\"c\":97.75},{\"o\":97.75,\"h\":97.83,\"l\":96.88,\"c\":96.95},{\"o\":96.95,\"h\":97.03,\"l\":96.08,\"c\":96.15},{\"o\":96.15,\"h\":96.23,\"l\":95.23,\"c\":95.3},{\"o\":95.3,\"h\":95.38,\"l\":94.43,\"c\":94.5},{\"o\":94.5,\"h\":94.58,\"l\":93.63,\"c\":93.7},{\"o\":93.7,\"h\":93.78,\"l\":92.78,\"c\":92.85},{\"o\":92.85,\"h\":92.93,\"l\":91.98,\"c\":92.05},{\"o\":92.05,\"h\":92.13,\"l\":91.13,\"c\":91.2},{\"o\":91.2,\"h\":91.28,\"l\":90.33,\"c\":90.4},{\"o\":90.4,\"h\":90.48,\"l\":89.95,\"c\":90}],\"visible\":12,\"describe\":\"LABX in a range between 97.90 and 100.35 for twelve candles, then a steady fall from 100.20 to 90.00 over thirteen candles.\"},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"In the range, lots bought at 99.00 and 98.00 sell 1.00 higher: three wins, +30.00.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":99,\"role\":\"neutral\",\"label\":\"Buy 99.00\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":98,\"role\":\"neutral\",\"label\":\"Buy 98.00\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":3,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"+10.00\",\"role\":\"up\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":7,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"+10.00\",\"role\":\"up\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":9,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"+10.00\",\"role\":\"up\"}]},{\"text\":\"Then a trend: at every 1.00 lower, the grid buys 10 more units.\",\"visible\":25,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":97,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":96,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":95,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":94,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":93,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":92,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":91,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":90,\"role\":\"neutral\"}]},{\"text\":\"At 90.00 it holds 100 units at an average of 94.50.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":94.5,\"role\":\"concept\",\"label\":\"Average 94.50\"},{\"kind\":\"bracket\",\"from\":90,\"to\":94.5,\"index\":24,\"label\":\"−4.50 per unit\",\"role\":\"down\"}]}]},\"concept\":\"grid-trading\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The open loss\",\"lines\":[\"The grid from the chart holds 100 units bought from 99.00 down to 90.00: average price 94.50.\",\"Price now: 90.00. Costs ignored.\",\"Open loss = 100 × (94.50 − 90.00) = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":450,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":1000,\"text\":\"That prices every unit at 100.00. They were bought on the way down, at an average of 94.50.\"},{\"value\":45,\"text\":\"That's 10 units' worth. The grid holds 100.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 450.00 open, and still no stop.\"},\"after\":\"It has put 9,450.00 into LABX, almost the whole 10,030.00 account, after a 10% fall. One more step down and it runs out of cash.\",\"concept\":\"grid-trading\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"What the research says\",\"text\":\"Taranto and Khan (2020) analysed grid trading as a gambler's-ruin problem and concluded that it 'will almost surely ruin an investment account in the long term', even though it can look like it beats the market over short stretches. Across market regimes, a grid profits in a range, until it doesn't, and is badly hurt by a trend.\"},{\"kind\":\"reveal\",\"title\":\"The equity curve\",\"prompt\":\"This is the grid's equity through the range: three wins, +30.00, barely a wobble. What does the trend from the chart above do to it?\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":10000,\"h\":10000,\"l\":10000,\"c\":10000},{\"o\":10000,\"h\":10000.5,\"l\":10000,\"c\":10000.5},{\"o\":10000.5,\"h\":10005,\"l\":10000.5,\"c\":10005},{\"o\":10005,\"h\":10010,\"l\":10005,\"c\":10010},{\"o\":10010,\"h\":10010,\"l\":10010,\"c\":10010},{\"o\":10010,\"h\":10010,\"l\":10009.5,\"c\":10009.5},{\"o\":10009.5,\"h\":10009.5,\"l\":10002,\"c\":10002},{\"o\":10002,\"h\":10020,\"l\":10002,\"c\":10020},{\"o\":10020,\"h\":10028.5,\"l\":10020,\"c\":10028.5},{\"o\":10028.5,\"h\":10030,\"l\":10028.5,\"c\":10030},{\"o\":10030,\"h\":10030,\"l\":10030,\"c\":10030},{\"o\":10030,\"h\":10030,\"l\":10030,\"c\":10030},{\"o\":10030,\"h\":10030,\"l\":10030,\"c\":10030},{\"o\":10030,\"h\":10030,\"l\":10026,\"c\":10026},{\"o\":10026,\"h\":10026,\"l\":10015,\"c\":10015},{\"o\":10015,\"h\":10015,\"l\":9998.5,\"c\":9998.5},{\"o\":9998.5,\"h\":9998.5,\"l\":9974.5,\"c\":9974.5},{\"o\":9974.5,\"h\":9974.5,\"l\":9942,\"c\":9942},{\"o\":9942,\"h\":9942,\"l\":9905,\"c\":9905},{\"o\":9905,\"h\":9905,\"l\":9862,\"c\":9862},{\"o\":9862,\"h\":9862,\"l\":9809.5,\"c\":9809.5},{\"o\":9809.5,\"h\":9809.5,\"l\":9754,\"c\":9754},{\"o\":9754,\"h\":9754,\"l\":9686,\"c\":9686},{\"o\":9686,\"h\":9686,\"l\":9616,\"c\":9616},{\"o\":9616,\"h\":9616,\"l\":9580,\"c\":9580}],\"visible\":12,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":10000,\"role\":\"neutral\",\"label\":\"Start 10,000.00\"}],\"describe\":\"The grid's equity, one candle per LABX candle. It creeps from 10,000.00 to 10,030.00 during the range, then slides to 9,580.00 during the trend.\"},\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It keeps creeping up\",\"feedback\":\"Every lot bought in the trend waits for a sale 1.00 higher that never comes.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It slides far below the start\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It stays flat until the grid stops\",\"feedback\":\"Open positions are marked at every close. Each step down adds 10 more units to the loss.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\",\"after\":\"From 10,030.00 to 9,580.00. The 450.00 open loss is 15 times what the range earned, and it grows with every step down.\\nA smooth curve only meant the trend hadn't come yet.\",\"concept\":\"grid-trading\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Where 5% is gone\",\"prompt\":\"Another grid: 5 units at every 1.00 step below 100.00, each sold at +1.00, no stop. How many steps can price fall in one move before the open loss passes 500.00, 5% of the account?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":1,\"max\":20,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"steps\",\"start\":1,\"answer\":15,\"tolerance\":0,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":1,\"to\":14,\"text\":\"Not yet. After 14 steps, at 86.00: 70 units, average 92.50, open loss 455.00.\"},{\"from\":16,\"to\":20,\"text\":\"It passed 500.00 one step earlier. At 85.00: 75 units, average 92.00, open loss 525.00.\"}]},\"reveal\":\"15 steps, at 85.00: 75 units at an average of 92.00, an open loss of 525.00. The loss grows faster than the fall: 225.00 after 10 steps, 950.00 after 20.\",\"concept\":\"grid-trading\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Grade F. A grid without stops is averaging down on a schedule: small, steady wins until a trend turns them into one large loss. Many prop firms ban grid and martingale strategies outright.\"}],\"check\":[\"q-1.8.2-1\",\"q-1.8.2-2\",\"q-1.8.2-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"A grid without stops adds units as price moves away: exposure grows as the idea fails.\",\"Open loss = units × (average price − current price): 100 × 4.50 = 450.00.\",\"A smooth equity curve hides open losses; one trend can erase many small wins.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 1.8.3: holy grails and survivorship bias, and how to read a performance claim before you believe it.\"}},\"1.8.3\":{\"unit\":\"1.8\",\"courseId\":\"1.8.3\",\"bridge\":\"In 1.8.2 a smooth equity curve hid a large open loss. Ads hide things too. In 0.2.6 luck alone made stars among coin-flippers, and 1.2.7 showed cherry-picked charts. Here you check a performance claim before you believe it.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"An ad says: 'Our system: 92% win rate, +340% last year.' What's the first thing to ask?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"How do I buy it?\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"How many trades, after which costs, and what was the largest loss?\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Which indicator does it use?\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. A claim means little until you know the sample, the costs and the worst loss. 92% says nothing without the size of the other 8%.\",\"concept\":\"performance-claims\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Questions for any claim\",\"text\":\"Before believing a [[backtest]] or a track record, ask: how many trades, over what period? After which costs? What were the largest loss and the [[max-drawdown|maximum drawdown]], the deepest fall from a peak? Simulated or real money? How many systems were tried before this one? And how did it compare with simply holding the market?\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"1,000 systems with no edge\",\"text\":\"Each system takes 100 trades, each with a 50% chance to win or lose 1R. None has an edge. The bars show how many of 1,000 such systems land in each band, on average. Showing only the top band is [[survivorship-bias|survivorship bias]].\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"+20R or more\",\"value\":28,\"role\":\"up\"},{\"label\":\"+10R to +18R\",\"value\":156,\"role\":\"accent\"},{\"label\":\"−8R to +8R\",\"value\":632,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"−10R to −18R\",\"value\":156,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"−20R or worse\",\"value\":28,\"role\":\"down\"}],\"steps\":[\"The ad shows one system with +20R over 100 trades. About 28 in 1,000 get there by luck.\",\"Run all 1,000 coin-flip systems: most finish within 8R of zero.\",\"As many lose 10R to 18R as make it.\",\"And 28 lose 20R or more. Nobody advertises those.\"]},\"concept\":\"performance-claims\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"92% and still losing\",\"lines\":[\"A system wins 92% of its trades at +0.5R.\",\"The other 8% lose 8R each: rare, but large.\",\"Expectancy = 0.92 × 0.5 − 0.08 × 8 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":-0.18,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"R\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":0.46,\"text\":\"That's the wins only. Subtract the losses: 0.08 × 8 = 0.64.\"},{\"value\":0.18,\"text\":\"Check the sign: 0.46 − 0.64 is below zero.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. −0.18R per trade, with a 92% win rate.\"},\"after\":\"The rare large loss has the same shape as martingale's and the grid's. A win rate without the size of the losses tells you nothing.\",\"concept\":\"performance-claims\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Even real edges shrink\",\"text\":\"McLean and Pontiff (2016) followed 97 return predictors published in finance journals. Their returns were 26% lower [[out-of-sample]] and 58% lower after publication. Even edges that were real shrink once they're known and traded. A rule of thumb: halve any published edge before counting on it, then subtract your costs.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Red flags\",\"prompt\":\"Match each red flag in an ad with what it hides.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Guaranteed returns\",\"right\":\"No edge is guaranteed\"},{\"left\":\"Only winning months shown\",\"right\":\"Cherry-picking\"},{\"left\":\"Backtest only, no live record\",\"right\":\"May be fitted to the past\"},{\"left\":\"Results without costs\",\"right\":\"Costs can erase the edge\"},{\"left\":\"Thousands of happy members\",\"right\":\"Survivors speak; losers don't\"}],\"columns\":[\"Red flag\",\"What it hides\"],\"concept\":\"performance-claims\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Ask who earns what when you buy. A seller paid per subscriber earns the same whether the system works or not.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"sample\",\"label\":\"How many trades\"},{\"id\":\"costs\",\"label\":\"Costs\"},{\"id\":\"worst\",\"label\":\"Worst loss and drawdown\"},{\"id\":\"selection\",\"label\":\"What wasn't shown\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"c1\",\"text\":\"'+18% in March!' One screenshot of one month: 3 trades.\",\"answer\":\"sample\",\"correct\":\"Right. Three trades in one good month can't separate skill from luck. Verdict: can't judge yet.\",\"rationales\":{\"costs\":\"Costs matter, but the first gap is bigger: 3 trades can't tell skill from luck.\",\"worst\":\"With 3 trades there's barely a worst case to show. The sample itself is the gap.\",\"selection\":\"It may be the best month, but even an honest 3-trade month is too small to judge.\"}},{\"id\":\"c2\",\"text\":\"'Our scalping bot: 2,400 trades this year, 61% winners, +31% before fees.'\",\"answer\":\"costs\",\"correct\":\"Right. 2,400 short trades pay 2,400 round trips. A result before fees can turn negative after them. Verdict: can't judge yet.\",\"rationales\":{\"sample\":\"2,400 trades is a decent sample. What's missing is what those round trips cost.\",\"worst\":\"Worth asking, but the bigger gap is written in the claim: '+31% before fees'.\",\"selection\":\"Possibly, but the claim itself admits the gap: the result is before fees.\"}},{\"id\":\"c3\",\"text\":\"'97% of our trades win. Steady income every week for two years.'\",\"answer\":\"worst\",\"correct\":\"Right. A 97% win rate fits a strategy with rare, huge losses, like a grid or martingale. Without the worst loss: red flag.\",\"rationales\":{\"sample\":\"Two years of weekly trades is plenty. The gap is the size of the 3% that lose.\",\"costs\":\"Costs matter, but a 97% win rate says nothing until you know how big the losses are.\",\"selection\":\"Possibly, but the first question for any very high win rate is the size of the losses.\"}},{\"id\":\"c4\",\"text\":\"'Our members' best results this year: +400%, +250%, +180%.'\",\"answer\":\"selection\",\"correct\":\"Right. The best three out of how many? The members who lost aren't in the ad. Survivorship: red flag.\",\"rationales\":{\"sample\":\"Three numbers, yes, but they were chosen. The gap is everyone who wasn't shown.\",\"costs\":\"Costs matter, but the bigger problem is that these are the best results, picked from many.\",\"worst\":\"Close: the worst results are missing, and so are all the ordinary ones. That's selection.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Four marketing claims. For each one, pick what's missing before you could judge it.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":4},\"correct\":\"All four. The gaps differ, but the habit is the same: before believing a number, ask what it leaves out.\",\"hints\":[\"Ask what you'd need to know before the number means anything.\",\"Few trades, results before fees, no losses mentioned, best results only: each points to one gap.\",\"A very high win rate hides the size of the losses; 'best results' hides everyone else.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":4},\"concept\":\"performance-claims\"},\"check\":[\"q-1.8.3-1\",\"q-1.8.3-2\",\"q-1.8.3-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Check any claim for sample size, costs, period, worst loss, selection and a benchmark.\",\"Of 1,000 systems with no edge, about 28 make +20R by luck. Ads show only those.\",\"Published edges fell about 58% after publication. Halve them, then subtract your costs.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 1.8.4: the people who sell those claims, signal sellers, scams and unregulated brokers, and how to check a licence.\"}},\"1.8.4\":{\"unit\":\"1.8\",\"courseId\":\"1.8.4\",\"bridge\":\"1.8.3 gave you questions for any performance claim. Now the people selling them: signal services, scams and unregulated brokers. In 0.1.5, 74% to 89% of EU retail CFD accounts lost money; here you check who holds your money and who gains when you lose.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"A broker offers 500:1 leverage, a 100% deposit bonus and a personal account manager. It is registered offshore. How many red flags?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"One\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Two\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Four\"}],\"answer\":\"c\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. All four: extreme leverage, a deposit bonus, a salesperson presented as service, and an offshore registration that gives you little protection.\",\"concept\":\"scam-red-flags\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Who licenses brokers\",\"text\":\"In the EU, national authorities license brokers under rules coordinated by ESMA; in Romania, that's the ASF. The UK has the FCA. The US has the SEC and FINRA, plus the CFTC and NFA for futures and forex. Check a licence on the regulator's own register, never only on the broker's website. Rules change, so recheck them.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Red flags and why\",\"prompt\":\"Match each red flag with the reason it matters.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Deposit bonus\",\"right\":\"Banned for EU retail CFD clients\"},{\"left\":\"Guaranteed monthly returns\",\"right\":\"No market return is guaranteed\"},{\"left\":\"Pressure to deposit more, now\",\"right\":\"A sales tactic, not advice\"},{\"left\":\"Slow or blocked withdrawals\",\"right\":\"A classic sign of fraud\"},{\"left\":\"Offshore licence only\",\"right\":\"Little protection for you\"}],\"columns\":[\"Red flag\",\"Why it matters\"],\"concept\":\"scam-red-flags\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"What protects your money?\",\"prompt\":\"Your regulated broker goes bust and its compensation scheme pays out. Does the scheme cover the 2,000.00 you lost on trades last month?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Yes, up to the scheme's limit\",\"feedback\":\"Schemes cover client money a failed firm can't return, within limits. Trading losses are never covered.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"No\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Only if the broker caused the loss\",\"feedback\":\"A compensation scheme isn't insurance against trading. It covers missing client money when a firm fails.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"reveal\":\"No. Regulated brokers must keep client money as [[segregated-funds|segregated funds]], apart from their own. If a firm fails, compensation schemes cover missing client money within limits: in the US, SIPC up to $500,000, of which $250,000 in cash; in the EU, at least €20,000. Trading losses are never covered. Check current limits.\",\"concept\":\"broker-due-diligence\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Who is on the other side?\",\"prompt\":\"Your broker runs a [[b-book]]: it takes the other side of your trades itself. You lose 100.00 on a trade. Who gains it?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Other traders in the market\",\"feedback\":\"That's an A-book broker, which passes your order on. A B-book broker is the market for you.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The broker\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nobody: the money just disappears\",\"feedback\":\"Trading is zero-sum before costs: what you lose, someone gains. Here, the broker.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"reveal\":\"The broker. An A-book broker passes your order to the market; a B-book broker takes the other side, so your loss is its revenue. That's a structural conflict of interest. It's legal and regulated, not automatically abusive, but you should know which model you're trading against.\",\"concept\":\"broker-due-diligence\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Signals, copy trading and paid challenges\",\"text\":\"[[signal-service|Signal services]] and copy trading show selected track records, with delays and with incentives to sell rather than perform. [[pump-and-dump|Pump-and-dump]] groups hype a coin or a stock, then sell to the buyers they attract. Paid 'funded trader' challenges earn from fees; pass rates are estimated at 5–10%, and those estimates are unaudited. More in 4.5.\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"If someone contacts you first with an opportunity, treat it as a scam until you've checked it yourself, on the regulator's own register.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"ok\",\"label\":\"Worth a closer look\"},{\"id\":\"licence\",\"label\":\"No real licence\"},{\"id\":\"bonus\",\"label\":\"Bonus and extreme leverage\"},{\"id\":\"withdraw\",\"label\":\"Withdrawal problems\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"text\":\"Broker A · Licensed by the FCA, and the FCA register lists it under the same name and website. Leverage up to 30:1 for retail clients, no bonuses, client money segregated.\",\"answer\":\"ok\",\"correct\":\"Right. Licence confirmed on the register, retail caps, no incentives. Worth a closer look: next, read its fees and execution model.\",\"rationales\":{\"licence\":\"The licence was checked on the FCA's own register, for the same name and website.\",\"bonus\":\"30:1 is the UK and EU retail cap on major currency pairs, and there's no bonus.\",\"withdraw\":\"Nothing in the profile mentions withdrawal problems.\"}},{\"id\":\"b\",\"text\":\"Broker B · 'Regulated' only by an offshore commission, with no EU, UK or US licence. Its website still signs up EU clients. Spreads from 0.0.\",\"answer\":\"licence\",\"correct\":\"Right. An offshore-only licence gives you little protection, whatever the spreads. Red flag.\",\"rationales\":{\"ok\":\"Tight spreads don't help if no serious regulator watches the firm. Check the licence first.\",\"bonus\":\"No bonus is mentioned. The problem is who regulates it.\",\"withdraw\":\"No complaints are mentioned yet. The licence is the problem.\"}},{\"id\":\"c\",\"text\":\"Broker C · Its EU company is licensed, but sign-up routes you to a sister company abroad that offers 500:1 and a 100% deposit bonus.\",\"answer\":\"bonus\",\"correct\":\"Right. The bonus and the 500:1 come from a company outside EU rules, so EU retail protections don't apply to you. Red flag.\",\"rationales\":{\"ok\":\"The licensed company isn't the one you'd sign with. Check which company holds your account.\",\"licence\":\"Partly: the licence covers a different company. The offer itself, 500:1 and a bonus, is the clearest flag.\",\"withdraw\":\"No withdrawal complaints are mentioned. Look at what it offers you to sign up.\"}},{\"id\":\"d\",\"text\":\"Broker D · Licensed in Cyprus and listed on the CySEC register. Many recent reviews say withdrawals take months or are refused.\",\"answer\":\"withdraw\",\"correct\":\"Right. A licence doesn't make withdrawals work. Blocked or very slow withdrawals are a classic warning sign. Red flag.\",\"rationales\":{\"ok\":\"The licence checks out, but many reports of refused withdrawals outweigh it.\",\"licence\":\"The licence is real and on the register. The problem is getting your money out.\",\"bonus\":\"No bonus or extreme leverage is mentioned. Read the reviews again.\"}},{\"id\":\"e\",\"text\":\"Broker E · Licensed by the ASF in Romania and on its register. Takes the other side of client trades (B-book), disclosed in its terms. Negative balance protection for retail clients.\",\"answer\":\"ok\",\"correct\":\"Right. A disclosed B-book is a conflict to know about, not a red flag on its own. Worth a closer look.\",\"rationales\":{\"licence\":\"The licence was found on the ASF's own register.\",\"bonus\":\"No bonus is mentioned, and negative balance protection is a retail safeguard.\",\"withdraw\":\"Nothing in the profile mentions withdrawal problems.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Five broker profiles, all made up. For each one, pick its biggest problem, or mark it worth a closer look.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":5},\"correct\":\"Done. Two of the five are worth a closer look, and even those need checking: fees, execution model and the register again before you deposit.\",\"hints\":[\"Start with the licence: is it on a real regulator's register, for the company you'd sign with?\",\"Then look at incentives and leverage, then at what clients say about getting money out.\",\"Two of the five profiles are worth a closer look.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":5},\"concept\":\"broker-due-diligence\"},\"check\":[\"q-1.8.4-1\",\"q-1.8.4-2\",\"q-1.8.4-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Check a licence on the regulator's own register, for the exact company you'd sign with.\",\"Red flags: guaranteed returns, bonuses, pressure, offshore-only licences, blocked withdrawals, unsolicited offers.\",\"A B-book broker gains when you lose: a legal conflict to know before you trade.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 1.8.5: the leverage illusion, why the leverage a broker allows isn't the leverage that hits your account.\"}},\"1.8.5\":{\"unit\":\"1.8\",\"courseId\":\"1.8.5\",\"bridge\":\"In 1.8.4, 500:1 leverage was a red flag. In 1.4.7 you saw leverage scale every move: at 5×, a 5% fall costs 25%. Here you separate the leverage a broker allows from the leverage you use, and see why a close-out isn't a stop.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Your broker offers 1:500. Trader A holds positions worth 2 times her equity; Trader B, 20 times his. Who carries more risk?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Both the same: the broker's 1:500 decides\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Trader B\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Trader A\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. The 1:500 is only a ceiling. B's positions are 20 times his equity, so a 5% move costs him the whole account; A loses 10%.\",\"concept\":\"effective-leverage\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Allowed versus used\",\"text\":\"The leverage a broker allows, such as 1:500, is a ceiling. What hits your account is [[effective-leverage|effective leverage]]: the value of your open positions divided by your [[equity]]. Two traders at the same broker can run 2× and 20×. The broker's number only tells you how far you could go.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"One formula\",\"lines\":[\"% of the account lost = % price move × effective leverage.\",\"At 2×, a 5% move against you costs 10% of the account.\",\"At 20×, a 5% move against you costs ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":100,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":5,\"text\":\"5% is the price move. Multiply it by the leverage: 20 × 5%.\"},{\"value\":20,\"text\":\"20 is the leverage. Multiply it by the 5% move.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 100%: the whole account, from an ordinary 5% move.\"},\"after\":\"The move that wipes you out is 100% ÷ effective leverage: 50% at 2×, 5% at 20×.\",\"concept\":\"effective-leverage\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"The leverage dial\",\"text\":\"The same trade, the same 3% move against you, at four sizes. The bars show the loss as a share of the account.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"1× effective\",\"value\":-3,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"3× effective\",\"value\":-9,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"10× effective\",\"value\":-30,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"30× effective\",\"value\":-90,\"role\":\"down\"}],\"unit\":\"%\",\"steps\":[\"At 1×, a 3% move costs 3% of the account.\",\"At 3×, 9%. At 10×, 30%.\",\"At 30×, 90%. Nothing about the trade changed, only its size.\"]},\"concept\":\"effective-leverage\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"How a close-out happens\",\"lines\":[\"Equity 5,000. You buy 1 lot of EUR/USD at 1.1000: 110,000 of currency.\",\"At 30:1 the broker requires margin of 110,000 ÷ 30 = 3,666.67.\",\"EU retail rules close you out at 50% of that: when equity falls to 1,833.33.\",\"Loss at the close-out = 5,000 − 1,833.33 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":3166.67,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"USD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":1833.33,\"text\":\"That's the equity left at the close-out. The loss is what's gone: 5,000 − 1,833.33.\"},{\"value\":3666.67,\"text\":\"That's the required margin. The close-out comes at half of it.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 3,166.67: 63% of the account.\"},\"after\":\"Price only had to fall 2.9%: 3,166.67 ÷ 110,000. A [[margin-call]] may warn you first; the [[margin-close-out]] doesn't ask. It closes at whatever price is there.\",\"concept\":\"margin-close-out\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"The rules for retail\",\"text\":\"For EU retail clients trading CFDs, leverage is capped from 30:1 on major currency pairs down to 2:1 on crypto. Positions are closed out at 50% of the required margin, per account, and [[negative-balance-protection|negative balance protection]] stops losses at the money in the account. The UK has similar rules. They limit damage; they don't size your trades.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Leverage and your planned risk\",\"prompt\":\"You size every trade from the stop to risk 1%. Your broker raises your maximum leverage from 1:30 to 1:500. Does your planned risk change?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Yes, it rises with the leverage\",\"feedback\":\"Your size comes from the stop and the 1% budget, not from the leverage. Same units, same loss at the stop.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"No\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"reveal\":\"No. Sizing decides the risk. Leverage only changes the margin set aside and how big a position you could open, which makes oversizing easy. And if price gaps past your stop, your exposure, not your plan, sets the loss.\",\"concept\":\"effective-leverage\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Leverage multiplies volatility, not edge. Using the maximum to grow a small account fast is grade F. MARKET//LAB uses no leverage; margin mechanics come back in 2.14.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"exposure\",\"goal\":\"Your broker allows 10×. A 4% move against you is ordinary. Set the position so that move costs exactly 20% of your 10,000.00 equity, then read your effective leverage.\",\"start\":{\"account\":10000,\"leverage\":10,\"position\":10000,\"move\":4},\"locked\":[\"account\",\"leverage\",\"move\"],\"success\":{\"field\":\"position\",\"eq\":50000},\"feedback\":[{\"when\":{\"field\":\"lossPct\",\"lt\":20},\"text\":\"At {leverageUsed}× a 4% move costs {lossPct:1}% of the account. Go bigger.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"lossPct\",\"gt\":20},\"text\":\"At {leverageUsed}× a 4% move costs {lossPct:1}% of the account. Go smaller.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 50,000.00 is 5× effective leverage: 4% × 5 = 20%. A 20% move would wipe the account out, with 10× still available.\",\"hints\":[\"% of the account lost = move × effective leverage.\",\"20% ÷ 4% = 5, so you need positions worth 5 times your equity.\",\"5 × 10,000.00 = 50,000.00.\"],\"showMe\":{\"position\":50000},\"concept\":\"effective-leverage\"},\"check\":[\"q-1.8.5-1\",\"q-1.8.5-2\",\"q-1.8.5-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Effective leverage = position value ÷ equity: 60,000.00 on 4,000.00 is 15×.\",\"The move that wipes out the account is 100% ÷ effective leverage: 5% at 20×.\",\"A close-out comes after most of the account is gone. Size from the stop instead.\"],\"next\":\"Next: the 1.8 checkpoint closes Level 1. Level 2 opens with unit 2.1, Candlesticks in context: the candle from 1.2, judged against its neighbours.\"}}}");
